Oblivion
========

Oblivion is an independent project developed by Exploita AI. The privacy
framework, controller, launcher and manager are distributed under the MIT
License. Oblivion is not affiliated with or endorsed by OpenAI, Anthropic,
Anomaly, Debian, or the maintainers of its other dependencies.

Selected terminal UI and theme utilities are retained from OpenCode, originally
published by Anomaly under the MIT License:

    https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode

The original copyright and license notices remain in LICENSE and retained
source files. The manager also uses selected Termcn-derived components; their
license and provenance are stored with that source and included in its build
assets. Clipboard and display components have their own adjacent license and
provenance records. The trusted local path picker uses shell-quote 1.12.0
(MIT, copyright 2013 James Halliday), from https://github.com/ljharb/shell-quote,
to decode one POSIX-quoted path without executing a shell or expanding variables.
Its original license is included in manager build assets and runtime bundles.

Oblivion 1.0 runs pinned, unmodified official agent executables. It no longer
ships an Oblivion fork of the coding agent. Their original notices and terms
are separate from the license for the Oblivion framework:

    Codex:       https://github.com/openai/codex
    Claude Code: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/legal-and-compliance
    OpenCode:    https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode

Claude Code is not covered by Oblivion's MIT license. Its documented commercial
integration conditions require an unmodified client, users authenticating with
their own accounts, and direct provider billing. Oblivion does not resell API
usage or authenticate users through an intermediary provider account.

Agent package versions, download URLs, integrity values and separate distribution
conditions are recorded in script/oblivion/install-agents.json. Pinned upstream
license and notice files are retained in script/oblivion/agent-notices and
installed under /usr/share/doc/oblivion-agents in the Safe image. The client
inventory identifies the actual installed architecture, executables and notices.
These records do not constitute acceptance of commercial terms or relicense
the clients. Debian packages, Python tool bundles and other dependencies retain
their own licenses; image inventories and bundled notices identify their contents.

